"Final design closure (2026-08-12, user): DEPENDENT STANCES AND RELATIONS. (1) Some stances have a target/dependency (grappled->grappler, lassoing/lassoed, chaining/chained, riding/ridden, climbing/climbed). Model: RELATIONS are typed entity-to-entity (or entity-to-object) links -- components carrying target entity refs, btree-indexed on the target so both directions are cheap. The RELATION and the STANCE CHANGE are separable: forcing an opposing stance is a property-gated CONTEST (a gargantuan body resists override and keeps rampaging/rolling/aiming), but the relation is recorded regardless, and the big creature's own actions can read it (shake-off gameplay while being climbed). (2) LIFECYCLE: when a dependency dies or leaves, the stance MUST end -- each dependent stance authors its EXIT explicitly: target stance (improvised/cowering/neutral) plus optional effects (fall damage when the mount dies). Authored exits, never silent unwinds; entity deletion consults the target-indexed relations to transition holders. (3) HYBRID cases (chained by one leg, chained by one arm but otherwise free) need NO hybrid stances: relations may CONSUME provider slots from a side's context -- a chain on one arm consumes hand x1 (single-handed actions survive by ordinary counted evaluation), a leg chain consumes gait/locomotion provision (movement techniques drop). The stance itself may be unchanged; the context shrank. This lands the unifying principle: bodies, armaments, stances, AND relations are all providers/consumers over one counted property vocabulary, and availability is always the same single evaluation. Runtime context changes (disarm, chains) are one category with one behavior. Design phase CLOSED pending the concrete data-model plan."